.TH CHECKMATE-POLICY 5 "@DATE@" "checkmate @VERSION@" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
checkmate-policy \- policy and waiver files of checkmate
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
name: Baseline
version: 1.0.0
include: [common.yml]
vars: { min_uid: 1000 }
defaults: { timeout: 30s, fail_on: medium }
snippets: { ... }
checks:
  \- id: SSH\-001
    name: Root login over SSH is disabled
    run: sshd \-T | awk '$1 == "permitrootlogin" {print $2}'
    expect: "== no"
waivers: [ ... ]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A policy is a YAML file.
Every check runs a command and compares its exit code, standard output or
standard error with an expectation.
Strings in a policy are Jinja2 templates with the variables and the facts
of the host; see
.B TEMPLATING
below.
.SH TOP-LEVEL KEYS
.TP
.B name
Title of the policy, shown in reports.
.TP
.B version
Version of the policy.
.TP
.B description
Free text.
.TP
.B include
List of policy files (relative to this file) whose checks, variables,
snippets and waivers are added.
Included files load first; values of the including file override theirs.
Include cycles are errors.
.TP
.B vars
Variables, available in templates.
Variables given with
.B \-e
on the command line override them.
.TP
.B vars_files
List of YAML files with more variables.
.TP
.B env
Environment variables for every command.
.TP
.B defaults
Defaults for the checks of this file:
.B timeout
(Go duration, default 30s),
.B severity
(default medium),
.B fail_on
(default low),
.B shell
and
.B when
(conditions added to every check of this file only).
.TP
.B snippets
Named commands, referenced from checks with
.BR "{ use: name, arg: value }" .
A snippet may have platform variants and uses
.B "{{ arg }}"
or
.BR "{{ args.key }}" .
.TP
.B checks
The list of checks.
.TP
.B waivers
Accepted deviations; see
.BR WAIVERS .
.SH CHECK KEYS
.TP
.B id
Unique identifier without spaces; required.
Inside a loop it must contain a template such as
.BR "PKG\-{{ item }}" .
.TP
.B name
Human-readable title; defaults to the id.
.TP
.B description
Free text, included in JSON reports.
.TP
.B tags
List of tags for
.B \-\-tags
and
.BR \-\-skip\-tags .
.TP
.B severity
.BR info ", " low ", " medium ", " high " or " critical .
Decides whether a failure counts (see
.B \-\-fail\-on
in
.BR checkmate (1))
and its weight in the score (0, 1, 3, 7, 10).
.TP
.B when
A Jinja2 expression or a list of expressions that must all be true, e.g.
.BR "facts.distro == \(dqdebian\(dq" .
Otherwise the check is skipped.
.TP
.B only_if
A command; the check is skipped if it exits non-zero.
.TP
.B loop
A list, a mapping (items with
.B key
and
.BR value )
or a template that evaluates to a list.
The check is expanded once per item, available as
.BR item .
.TP
.B loop_run
A command; the check is expanded once per line of its output.
The item is data from the host, so
.B run
and
.B only_if
cannot use the
.B item
template and read the environment variable
.B CHECKMATE_ITEM
instead.
No output gives one result N/A, a failing command one result ERROR.
.BR validate ", " list " and " diff
do not run the command and show the check once, with
.B {item}
as the item.
.TP
.B no_log
If true, the command, its output, the expectation and the messages that
quote them are kept out of every report and off the console.
For checks that handle a secret.
The status and the exit code remain.
.TP
.B register
A name for the outcome of the check.
Checks defined later can use it in
.BR when :
.BR name.passed ", " name.failed ", " name.status ", " name.ran " and " name.rc .
The output of the check is not available; a registered check only
decides whether other checks run.
.TP
.B run
The command; required.
A string, a mapping of platform variants, or a snippet reference.
.TP
.B expect
The expectation; required.
See
.BR EXPECT .
.TP
.B as
How values are compared:
.B auto
(default),
.BR number ,
.B version
or
.BR string .
.TP
.B allow_rc
Exit codes accepted when
.B expect
looks at the output; default
.BR [0] .
.B any
accepts every exit code except 126 and 127.
.TP
.B timeout
Go duration, e.g. 500ms, 30s, 5m.
.TP
.B shell
.BR bash ", " sh ", " zsh ", " pwsh ", " powershell " or " cmd .
.TP
.B rationale
Why the check matters: the risk it guards against.
Shown with
.B \-vv
and in the reports.
.TP
.B remediation
How to fix a failure; shown with
.B \-vv
and in the reports.
.TP
.B reference
A link to background documentation.
.SH PLATFORM VARIANTS
.B run
and
.B only_if
may map platform keys to commands.
checkmate uses the first key that matches the host, in the order of its
platform keys: the distribution
.RB ( rhel ", " rocky ", " debian ", " ubuntu ", " sles ", " macos ", " windows ,
\&...), the distributions it is like, the operating system
.RB ( linux ", " darwin ", " windows ),
.B unix
for every non-Windows system, and
.BR default .
A key may list several platforms separated by commas, e.g.
.BR "rhel,suse:" .
Without a matching variant the check is N/A.
.SH EXPECT
An expectation is evaluated against the exit code and the output.
Comparisons use standard output, trimmed; regular expressions are RE2 with
multi-line mode.
.TP
.BR true ", " false
The exit code is 0, or is not 0.
.TP
.BI "== " value ", != " value
Equal, not equal.
.TP
.BI "> " n ", >= " n ", < " n ", <= " n ", !> " n ", !< " n
Numeric comparison, or version comparison with
.BR "as: version" .
.TP
.BI "~ " regex ", !~ " regex
Matches, does not match.
.TP
.BI "in [" a ", " b "], !in [" a ", " b ]
Is one of, is none of the values.
.TP
.BR empty ", " !empty
No output, some output.
.TP
.BI "{ rc: " condition " }"
A condition on the exit code, e.g.
.BR "{ rc: \(dqin [0, 100]\(dq }" .
.TP
.BI "{ stderr: " condition " }, { lines: " condition " }"
A condition on standard error, or on the number of output lines.
.TP
.BI "[ " a ", " b " ]"
A list: all conditions must hold.
.TP
.BI "{ any: [ " a ", " b " ] }, { not: " condition " }"
At least one holds; the condition does not hold.
.TP
.BI "{ each: " c " }, { some: " c " }, { none: " c " }"
Every line, at least one line, no line satisfies
.IR c .
Regular expressions see the untrimmed line.
.TP
.BI "{ if: " c ", then: " a ", else: " b " }"
A conditional expectation.
.TP
.BI "{ cases: [{ if: " c ", then: " a " }, ...], else: " b " }"
The first case whose condition holds decides.
If none does,
.B else
decides; without it the expectation fails.
.SH TEMPLATING
Strings are rendered with Jinja2 (gonja) in strict mode: an undefined
variable is an error.
Available are the variables of the policy, the extra variables, the loop
item and
.BR facts :
.BR facts.os ", " facts.arch ", " facts.hostname ", " facts.distro ,
.BR facts.distro_like ", " facts.distro_name ", " facts.version ,
.BR facts.version_major ", " facts.kernel " and " facts.is_root
(effective UID 0; on Windows: running elevated).
.PP
Comments use
.B "{## ##}"
instead of
.BR "{# #}" ,
so that shell code such as
.B "${#array}"
stays intact.
.B "{% raw %}"
protects text that contains template syntax.
.SH WAIVERS
A waiver accepts the failure of checks for a documented reason.
Waivers are listed under
.B waivers
in a policy, or in a waiver file passed with
.BR \-\-waivers ,
which contains only
.B include
and
.BR waivers :
.PP
.RS
.nf
include: [common.yml]
waivers:
  \- id: SSH\-PORT
    reason: Port 2222 by decision of the network team
    until: 2027\-06\-30
    when: facts.hostname == "web01"
  \- id: [CIS\-RHEL9\-1.1.2.*]
    reason: /tmp is a tmpfs managed by systemd
    skip: true
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B id
Check ID or glob pattern, or a list of them; required.
.TP
.B reason
Why the deviation is accepted; required and shown in every report.
.TP
.B until
Last day the waiver applies (YYYY-MM-DD).
Afterwards the check counts again and the report shows a notice.
.TP
.B when
Conditions like those of a check; the waiver applies only if all hold.
.TP
.B skip
.B true
does not run the check at all.
.PP
The first matching waiver wins.
A file comes before the files it includes, a later
.B \-\-waivers
file before an earlier one, and
.B \-\-waivers
files before the waivers of the policy.
.SH SECURITY
When checkmate runs as root it refuses policy and waiver files that other
users can modify, because their commands run as root and their waivers can
hide findings.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR checkmate (1)
.PP
Reference documentation: https://rtulke.github.io/checkmate/reference/
